<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Nauru</h1><p class="st-meta">1968 CE – present · capital Yaren</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The world's third-smallest country, a phosphate island independent from Australia since 1968, whose mining wealth was lost and which relies on Australian aid.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/territory-of-nauru">Territory of Nauru</a> was administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">League of Nations mandate</a> and then a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> trusteeship from 1947. Nauru became self-governing in January 1966, and the Nauru Independence Act 1967 allowed independence on 31 January 1968, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammer-deroburt">Hammer DeRoburt</a> as the first president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The government bought the assets of the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phosphate">Phosphate</a> Commission in 1967, and in June 1970 control passed to the locally owned Nauru Phosphate Corporation. Income from the mines made Nauruans among the richest people in the world, but the viable reserves were exhausted in the 1990s. Mismanagement of the royalty trust, which shrank from A$1.3 billion in 1991 to A$138 million in 2002, brought serious financial trouble through the 2000s, and the economy gradually recovered in the 2010s. In 1989 Nauru took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a> over mining damage, ending in an out-of-court settlement.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Nauru is a republic with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary system</a>. The 19-member parliament elects the president from among its members, and most members are independents. A 2021 referendum barred naturalised citizens and their descendants from becoming parliamentarians. Since 2001 Nauru has accepted Australian aid in exchange for hosting the Nauru Regional Processing Centre, and David Adeang is the president.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nauru.jpg" alt="Nauru" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: Skimel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nauru.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1968 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yaren</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hammer DeRoburt, David Adeang</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christian (Nauru Congregational Church, Catholic Church, Assemblies of God)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Nauruan, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nauru">Nauru</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Nauru</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1997&amp;focus=nauru" aria-label="Nauru on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Nauru lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g18000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3892 5045h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3892 5045h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1997&amp;focus=nauru">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
